@<basic@:light module@:C Structure@/include flags@=
#define NDEBUG
@
@<state@:light module@:C Structure@/include flags@=
#define NDEBUG
@
@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/include flags@=
#define NDEBUG
#define SHOW_COMPILE_INFO
@

@<introduction of litar@=
Programmers have a lot of text to deal with. They write code,
they write code to compile code, they write document, they write
code to format document, they write code to parse whatever text,
they even spell commands to control computer through shells.
Basically, all work that programmers do is writing text.

The traditional unit of text is file, text of different types
is saved in seperate files, but sometimes, it might take
too much energy to create a new file. Like, you want to
insert a build time information to your code whenever you build
your program. To do that, you might write a simple sed script and
put that to Makefile. Here is a problem, sed operate on the whole
file, so you have to make sure that the sed script won't influence the other
parts of the file. To solve that problem, you might move that part of code to a new file,
use a function to encapsulate it or simply store the information
in a macro or a variable and pass back to the original file.
You can see all the extra efforts made here.

litar is created to operate on a smaller unit of text, which is called chunk.
With litar, you can gather text of whatever type to a single file
and describe the relations between chunks at ease. Some relations
are hard or cumbersome to implement if between files.
What's more, follow the spirit of literate programming,
the readability is supposed to be improved a lot.
A such file is called a litar archive, the name litar is by combining
literate and archive.
@

@<reference of syntax@=
Because a litar archive is supposed to contain text of all types, the syntax of
itself should be as simple as possible. The syntax of a litar archive is built upon
control characters, which are @@ followed by a non-alphabetic character.

To fully describe all concepts, we shall introduce another term, block.
In the outermost, there are blocks interleaved with comments.
Each block consists of two parts: the name of a chunk and contents of current block.

```
Some comments.

@@<chunk1@@=
    The contents of block1.
@@

Some other comments

@@<chunk2@@=
    The contents of block2.
@@

@@<chunk1@@=
    The contents of block3.
@@

```

We say here that chunk1 is extended by block1 and block3, chunk2 is extended by block2.

There is another relation between chunk and block, include.

```
Some comments.

@@<chunk1@@=
    The contents of block1.
@@

Some other comments

@@<chunk2@@=
    The contents of block2.
    @@<chunk1@@>
    The contents of block2.
@@

```

The contents of block2 contains a reference to chunk1, we say block2 includes chunk1
at the position of the reference to chunk1.

The definition for the content of a chunk is the expanded content of all
blocks appended to the chunk concatenated.
The definition for the expanded content of a block is the content of the block
that all references to chunk are replaced by the content of the corresponding chunk.

It's a recursive procedure to evaluate the content of a chunk.
So a block can not includes the chunk that it extends.

The syntax above decribes some common concepts that all literate programming tools have.
litar has some useful extensions, which make it a general purpose tool
for text processing rather than a deliberate literate programming tool.
As a result, litar will not take in weaving to its kernel.

The major extension is user defined filters.
Noweb has some similar designs, but litar makes use of filters even more.

The following simple program explains the usage of filter in litar.

```
@@<hello.c@@=
#include "stdio.h"
int main() {
    @@<print hello world@@|exaggeratedly@@>
    return 0;
}
@@

@@<print hello world@@=
printf("Hello, world!\n");
@@

@@<exaggeratedly@@=#!/bin/sh
sed 's/world/WORLD/'
@@
```

The final output of this program is "Hello, WORLD!". The creation
of a chunk is pretty easy compared to files, so is the operation to
the chunk. The operation takes effect through pipes,
and itself is nothing more than a chunk. An operation like this is called filter.
Filter and pipe have the same meaning as in the context of Unix shell.

Filters are scripts that the first line must be a shebang,
so litar can support all script languages without extra effort.

Filters can also make effect on blocks. The following program is same with the above.

```
@@<hello.c@@=
#include "stdio.h"
int main() {
    @@<print hello world exaggeratedly@@>
    return 0;
}
@@

@@<print hello world exaggeratedly@@=
printf("Hello, world!\n");
@@|exaggeratedly@@

@@<exaggeratedly@@=#!/bin/sh
sed 's/world/WORLD/'
@@
```

The two relations are extended to,

1. a chunk is extended by a block transformed by filters;
2. a block include a chunk transformed by filters
   at the position of reference to the chunk.

The whole design of litar is around this extension, all other stuffs are
supplements to this, and this is the hardest part.
Filters are executed by litar, so litar has to
face the complication of building a correct process. For example,
which directory should be the working directory of filters,
how to decide what files a filter can access, etc.

It's ideal if a filter just use 3 files, stdin, stdout and stderr,
and do nothing more than transform input from stdin to stdout,
occasionally report error through stderr. In reality,
it's too restrictive. Like Bourne Shell, it can not do anything meaningful
without accessing tools provided by the underlying operating system.
Even did we limit the choices of outside programs to a fixed set,
then how to explain, let's say, sed's ability to use script files.
So, to simply regard filters as executables and run them under
current directory is enough? No, what if the invocated sed command want
to make use of a script file written in the litar archive?
Should we extract the file from the litar archive manually then run the sed command?
It's too cumbersome, not at ease at all!

The author of litar does not want to limit the powerful flexibility of Unix tools.
So unlike traditional literate programming tools, litar has syntax to introduce
the concept of files explicitly to the kernel of it. This is done by using @@[ and @@(.

```
@@[hello.c@@=
#include "stdio.h"
int main() {
    @@<print hello world@@|exaggeratedly@@>
    return 0;
}
@@

@@<print hello world@@=
printf("Hello, world!\n");
@@

@@[sed_scripts/bigger_world.sed@@=
s/world/WORLD/
@@

@@(run_bigger_world.sh@@=#!/bin/sh
sed -f sed_scripts/bigger_world.sed
@@

@@<exaggeratedly@@=#!/bin/sh
./run_bigger_world.sh
@@
```

There are three files in this example, "hello.c", "sed_scripts/bigger_world.sed"
and "exaggeratedly". The difference between @@[ and @@( is that @@( specifies
an executable file but @@['s is a regular file.
Filters are executed in an environment so that these three
files can be accessed.
It's worthy to mention that a file in a litar archive is also a chunk.

The discussion above implies that a litar archive has a file system inside.
To be compatible with Unix programming environment, litar uses a technology called
Filesystem in Userspace (FUSE), which allows litar to create a custom file system
and mount it. So filters can access the files in a litar archive just like
other files in the operating system.

There's another important extension for reusability of code. Let's say, a chunk
defines a general structure for a C program. Then we want to write two seperated
C program in one litar archive. The final result might look like,

```
@@<general structure for C program@@=
@@<includes@@>
int main(int argc, char *argv)
{
    @@<main body of C program@@>
}
@@

@@[program1.c@@=
@@<general structure for C program@@>
@@

@@[program2.c@@=
@@<general structure for C program@@>
@@
```

It does not work because all extending to the @@<general structure for C program@@>
reflects on both programs. litar introduce specialization of chunks.

```
@@<general structure for C program@@=
@@<includes@@>
int main(int argc, char *argv)
{
    @@<main body of C program@@>
}
@@

@@[program1.c@@=
@@<program1@@:general structure for C program@@>
@@

@@[program2.c@@=
@@<program2@@:general structure for C program@@>
@@

@@<program1@@:includes@@=
#include <stdio.h>
@@

@@<progarm1@@:main body of C program@@=
printf("Hello from program1\n");
@@
```

Extending through the specialized name does not affect the original chunk.

When a litar archive becomes bigger and bigger, despite that the name of chunks
is usually a long sentence, names might conflict.
So litar supports modulizing.

```
@@- Module 1

@@<chunk@@=
    The contents.
@@

@@- Module 2

@@<chunk@@=
    @@<Module 1@@/chunk@@>
@@
```

litar does not stop users from modifying chunks of other modules, in fact,
it's a powerful way to interact with other modules, but use this feature
with caution.

Besides the named module, there's a anonymous module. If there's no @@-,
or there's no module name specified after @@-, the chunks belong to this
anonymous module.

litar's internal file system makes use of this module system.

```
/
    module 1
        directory 1
            file 1
        directory 2
        ...
    module 2
    ...
```

A filter is executed under the directory of the module it belongs to.
A module is simply a directory in some way, so litar also allows importing
outside directories.

```
@@+ toolset

@@<filter@@=#!/bin/sh
../toolset/tool
@@
```

The final piece of code reusability is @@., which is indroduced before.

```
@@<chunk@@=
    @@<module in archive@@/chunk@@>
@@

@@. archive.la
```

@@. is not only for including litar archives.
It can be used to import any text content at any beginning of a line.
The following is also legal.

```
@@<chunk@@=
    @@<module in archive@@/chunk@@>
@@. content.txt
@@

```

Besides the control characters that describes the structure of a litar archive,
there are some other control characters. They are commands, which influence
the process of dealing with litar archive. For example, @@. receives an argument
of file name, and inserts the content of that file at the place of @@..
@

The complete implementation of litar is divided to two parts, front end and back end.
The front end follows the common pattern of a command line tool.
It parses options and arguments, executes, then exits.
The back end is a server that makes use of libfuse3.

@[litar.c@=
@<litar@:light@:C Structure@/executable@>
@

@[litarfs.c@=
@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/executable@>
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/includes@=
#include <stdio.h>
#include <limits.h>
#include "light.h"
#include "state.h"
@

They communicate through the mounted file system.
Other programs can communicate with the back end too by this method.

The back end adopts the framework provided by libfuse3.

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/include flags@=
#define FUSE_USE_VERSION FUSE_MAKE_VERSION(3, 14)
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/includes@=
#include <fuse.h>
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/data types@=
struct litarfs_options {
    @<litarfs user options@>
};
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/global variables@=
static struct litarfs_options user_options;
static struct fuse_opt litarfs_option_specs[] = {
    @<litarfs user option specs@>
};
static const struct fuse_operations litarfs_oper = {
    @<litarfs operations@>
};
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/macros@=
#define LITARFS_OPER(op) .op = litarfs_##op
@

@<litarfs@:C Structure@/invocation syntax@=
{
    name => 'litarfs',
    usages => [
        {
            options => [@<command line options for "litarfs MOUNT_POINT"@>],
            arguments => ['MOUNT_POINT'],
        },
    ],
}
@

@<litarfs user options@=@<Text Processing@/basic perl settings@>

my $syntax = @<litarfs@:C Structure@/invocation syntax@>;

sub option_to_c_decl (_) {
    my $opt = shift;
    my $c_decl = '';
    if ($opt->{argument}) {
        $c_decl .= 'const char *';
    } else {
        $c_decl .= 'int ';
    }
    $c_decl . lc($opt->{long_opt}) . ';'
}

my @usages = @@{$syntax->{usages}};
my $options_decl = join "\n", map option_to_c_decl, @@{$usages[0]->{options}};
print $options_decl;

@|Basics@/evaluate@

@<litarfs user option specs@=@<Text Processing@/basic perl settings@>

my $syntax = @<litarfs@:C Structure@/invocation syntax@>;

sub option_to_opt_spec (_) {
    my $opt = shift;
    my $opt_spec = '';
    my $p_arg = '';
    if ($opt->{argument}) {
        $p_arg = '=%s';
    }
    $opt_spec .=
        "{\"--$opt->{long_opt}$p_arg\", offsetof(struct litarfs_options, $opt->{long_opt}), 1},";

    if ($opt->{argument}) {
        $p_arg = ' %s';
    }
    if ($opt->{opt}) {
        $opt_spec .=
            "\n{\"-$opt->{opt}$p_arg\", offsetof(struct litarfs_options, $opt->{long_opt}), 1},";
    }
    $opt_spec
}

my @usages = @@{$syntax->{usages}};
my $option_specs = join "\n", map option_to_opt_spec, @@{$usages[0]->{options}};
print $option_specs;

@|Basics@/evaluate@

@<command line options for "litarfs MOUNT_POINT"@=
{
    long_opt => "help",
    opt => "h",
    hide => 1,
    description => "Show help",
},
{
    long_opt => "version",
    opt => "V",
    hide => 1,
    description => "Show version",
},
@

@<litarfs@:Light@/main body@=
struct fuse_args args = FUSE_ARGS_INIT(argc, argv);
new_resource(&args, (void (*)(void *))fuse_opt_free_args);

@<set default values for "user_options"@>

if (fuse_opt_parse(&args, &user_options, litarfs_option_specs, NULL) < 0) {
    fprintf(stderr, "Error when parsing arguments\n");
    longjmp(exit_main, 1);
}

int to_mount_file_system = 1;

if (user_options.help) {
    printf("@<litarfs@:C Structure@/command line usage@>");
    fuse_opt_add_arg(&args, "--help");
    to_mount_file_system = 0;
}

if (user_options.version) {
    @<print version information@>
    fuse_opt_add_arg(&args, "--version");
    to_mount_file_system = 0;
}

if (to_mount_file_system) {
    @<build context for "fuse_main"@>
    fuse_opt_add_arg(&args, "-s");
} else {
    args.argv[0][0] = '\0';
}

returned = fuse_main(args.argc, args.argv, &litarfs_oper, NULL);
@

@<command line options for "litarfs MOUNT_POINT"@=
{
    long_opt => "archive",
    opt => "a",
    argument => "ARCHIVE",
    description => "Specify the litar archive",
},
@

@<set default values for "user_options"@=
user_options.archive = strdup("archive.la");
@

@<build context for "fuse_main"@=
@


Before we get into "fuse_main", we should build a proper context for it.
The context should contain all information needed to build a file system.

Concepts illustrated in the previous chapters
should be represented before the details of implementation.

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/data type declarations@=
typedef struct mappings_archive *Archive;
typedef struct mappings_module *Module;
typedef struct mappings_chunk *Chunk;
typedef struct mappings_block *Block;
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/data types@=
struct mappings_archive {
    @<mappings from archive@>
};

struct mappings_module {
    @<mappings from module@>
};

struct mappings_chunk {
    @<mappings from chunk@>
};

struct mappings_block {
    @<mappings from block@>
};
@

@<mappings from archive@=
HashTable modules;
List blocks;
@

There are some named modules and an anonymous module.
The anonymous module is a module whose name is an empty string.

@<mappings from module@=
const char *name;
Archive archive;
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
Module module_of_name_in_archive(const char *name, Archive ar)
{
    Module mod;
    mod = get_from_hash_table(ar->modules, name);
    if (!mod) {
        NEW0(mod);
        mod->name = name;
        mod->archive = ar;
        put_to_hash_table(ar->modules, name, mod);
    }
    return mod;
}
@

A module contains chunks.

@<mappings from module@=
HashTable chunk_name_to_chunk;
@

A chunk belongs to a module, a chunk has a name.

@<mappings from chunk@=
Module module;
const char *name;
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
Chunk chunk_of_name_in_module(const char *name, Module mod)
{
    if (!mod->chunk_name_to_chunk) {
        mod->chunk_name_to_chunk = new_string_hash_table(HASH_TABLE_DEFAULT_SIZE);
    }
    Chunk chk = get_from_hash_table(mod->chunk_name_to_chunk, name);
    if (!chk) {
        Chunk chk;
        NEW0(chk);
        chk->name = normalize(name);
        chk->module = mod;
        put_to_hash_table(mod->chunk_name_to_chunk, chk->name, chk);
    }
    return chk;
}
@

A chunk is specialized by labels.

@<mappings from archive@=
List collection_of_labels;
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
static int equal_labels(List lbls1, List lbls2)
{
    List l1, l2;
    int ret = 1;

    RENAME(lbls1, l1);
    RENAME(lbls2, l2);

    while (l1 && l2) {
        ret = ret && (car(l1) == car(l2));
        if (!ret) {
            break;
        }
        l1 = cdr(l1);
        l2 = cdr(l2);
    }
    if (l1 || l2) {
        ret = 0;
    }
    return ret;
}

List get_labels_from(List lbls, Archive ar)
{
    if (!lbls) {
        return lbls;
    }

    List _lbls;
    FOREACH (_lbls, ar->collection_of_labels) {
        if (equal_labels(lbls, _lbls)) {
            return _lbls;
        }
    }
    _lbls = cons(car(lbls), get_labels_from(cdr(lbls), ar));
    push(_lbls, &ar->collection_of_labels);
    return _lbls;
}
@

@<mappings from chunk@=
HashTable labels_to_blocks;
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
List blocks_of_chunk_specialized_by(Chunk chk, List lbls)
{
    if (!chk->labels_to_blocks) {
        return NULL;
    }
    lbls = get_labels_from(lbls, chk->module->archive);
    List blks = get_from_hash_table(chk->labels_to_blocks, lbls);
    return blks;
}

void block_extend_chunk_specialized_by(Block blk, Chunk chk, List lbls)
{
    if (!chk->labels_to_blocks) {
        chk->labels_to_blocks = new_pointer_hash_table(HASH_TABLE_DEFAULT_SIZE);
    }
    lbls = get_labels_from(lbls, chk->module->archive);
    List blks = blocks_of_chunk_specialized_by(chk, lbls);
    push(blk, &blks);
    put_to_assoc_table(chk->labels_to_blocks, lbls, blks);
}
@

A reference to chunk is consists of a chunk and a list of labels.

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/data type declarations@=
typedef struct {Chunk chunk; List labels;} ChunkRef;
@

@<mappings from block@=
ChunkRef chunk_ref;
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
List blocks_of_chunk_ref(ChunkRef ckr)
{
    return blocks_of_chunk_specialized_by(ckr.chunk, ckr.labels);
}

void block_extend_chunk_ref(Block blk, ChunkRef ckr)
{
    blk->chunk_ref = ckr;
    block_extend_chunk_specialized_by(blk, ckr.chunk, ckr.labels);
}
@

The contents of a block may either be a reference to the original text
or a reference to a chunk and a list of filters.

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/data types@=
typedef struct {
    enum {
        BLOCK_CONTENT_TEXT,
        BLOCK_CONTENT_CHUNK_REF,
    } type;
    union {
        const char *text;
        struct {
            ChunkRef chunk_ref;
            List filters;
        } chunk;
    } value;
} *BlockContent;
@

A block contains a list of contents defined before.

@<mappings from block@=
List contents;
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
void block_contain_text(Block blk, const char *txt)
{
    BlockContent bc;

    NEW0(bc);
    bc->type = BLOCK_CONTENT_TEXT;
    bc->value.text = txt;
    push(bc, &blk->contents);
}

void block_include_chunk_ref_with_filters(Block blk, ChunkRef ckr, List flts)
{
    BlockContent bc;

    NEW0(bc);
    bc->type = BLOCK_CONTENT_CHUNK_REF;
    bc->value.chunk.chunk_ref = ckr;
    bc->value.chunk.filters = flts;
    push(bc, &blk->contents);
}
@

A block extending a chunk might be transformed by filters.

@<mappings from block@=
List filters;
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
void block_is_transformed_by(Block blk, Chunk flt)
{
    push(flt, &blk->filters);
}
@

We can assign the comment before a block to that block.
@<mappings from block@=
const char *comment;
@

Some chunks may be labeled as files,
a file system can be built from these file paths.

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/data type declarations@=
typedef enum {
    CHUNK_TYPE_NORMAL,
    CHUNK_TYPE_REGULAR_FILE,
    CHUNK_TYPE_EXECUTABLE,
} ChunkType;
@

@<mappings from chunk@=
ChunkType type;
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/data types@=
typedef struct {
    enum {
        NODE_DIRECTORY,
        NODE_REGULAR,
        NODE_EXECUTABLE,
    } type;
    union {
        List nodes;
        Chunk chunk;
    } value;
    const char *name;
} *Node;
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/global variables@=
Node root_node;
@

@<litarfs@:Light@/initializations of main context@=
NEW(root_node);
root_node->type = NODE_DIRECTORY;
root_node->value.nodes = NULL;
root_node->name = atom_str("");
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
Node node_of_name_under_node(const char *file, Node node)
{
    Node nd;
    if (node->type != NODE_DIRECTORY) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Node %s is not directory\n", node->name);
        longjmp(exit_main, 1);
    }
    FOREACH(nd, node->value.nodes) {
        if (strcmp(file, nd->name) == 0) {
            return nd;
        }
    }
    return NULL;
}

Node node_of_path(Node root, const char *path)
{
    char *path_t = NULL;
    Node node = root;
    int i = 0, f = i, l = strlen(path);

    UNDER(
        STRDUP(path, path_t);

        if (path[0] == '/') {
            ++i;
            ++f;
        }

        while (f < l) {
            if (path_t[i] == '/' || path_t[i] == '\0') {
                path_t[i] = '\0';
                if ((node = node_of_name_under_node(path_t+f, node)) == NULL) {
                    break;
                }
                f = i+1;
            }
            ++i;
        }
    );

    return node;
}

char *path_of_chunk(Chunk chk)
{
    char *ret;
    UNDER(
        Str path;
        path = new_str();
        if (chk->module != module_of_name(chk->module->modules, "")) {
            str_extend(path, chk->module->name);
            str_extend(path, "/");
        }
        str_extend(path, chk->name);
        ESCAPE(STRDUP(raw_string(path), ret));
    );
    return ret;
}

Node node_of_chunk(Node root, Chunk chk)
{
    Node nd;
    UNDER(nd = node_of_path(root, path_of_chunk(chk)));
    return nd;
}

void there_is_module_in_file_system(Node root, Module mod)
{
    Node nd;

    NEW0(nd);
    nd->type = NODE_DIRECTORY;
    nd->name = mod->name;
    nd->value.nodes = NULL;
    push(nd, &root->value.nodes);
}

void chunk_is_file(Node root, Chunk chk)
{
    char *path;
    Node node = root, nd;
    int i = 0, f = i, l;

    UNDER(
        path = path_of_chunk(chk);
        l = strlen(path);

        while (f < l) {
            if (path[i] == '/') {
                path[i] = '\0';
                if ((nd = node_of_name_under_node(path+f, node)) == NULL) {
                    ESCAPE(
                        NEW(nd);
                        nd->type = NODE_DIRECTORY;
                        nd->name = atom_str(path+f);
                        nd->value.nodes = NULL;
                        push(nd, &node->value.nodes);
                    );
                }
                node = nd;
                f = i+1;
            } else if (path[i] == '\0') {
                if ((nd = node_of_name_under_node(path+f, node)) == NULL) {
                    ESCAPE(
                        NEW(nd);
                        nd->type = NODE_REGULAR;
                        nd->name = atom_str(path+f);
                        nd->value.chunk = chk;
                        push(nd, &node->value.nodes);
                    );
                }
                break;
            }
            ++i;
        }
    );
}

void chunk_is_executable(Node root, Chunk chk)
{
    chunk_is_file(root, chk);

    Node nd;
    nd = node_of_chunk(root, chk);
    nd->type = NODE_EXECUTABLE;
}
@

From the definitions above, we can define the content of chunk
and expanded content of block.

litar use a stack to detect recursive including and report it as a severe error.
@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/global variables@=
List stack_of_chunks_in_tangling = NULL;
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
int chunk_is_in_tangling(Chunk chk)
{
    Chunk chk_t;
    FOREACH (chk_t, stack_of_chunks_in_tangling) {
        if (chk_t == chk) {
            return 1;
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/function declarations@=
const char *content_of_chunk_with_labels(Chunk chk, List lbls);
const char *content_of_chunk_ref(ChunkRef ckr);
const char *expanded_content_of_block_with_labels(Block blk, List lbls);
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
const char *
content_of_chunk_with_labels(Chunk chk, List lbls)
{
    if (chunk_is_in_tangling(chk)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Recursively include \"%s\"\n", chk->name);
        longjmp(exit_main, 1);
    }

    char *ret;

    UNDER(
        Str cc = new_str();
        List remains = lbls, passeds = NULL;
        do {
            List blks;

            UNDER(
                reverse(&passeds);
                blks = copy_list(blocks_of_chunk_specialized_by(chk, passeds));
                reverse(&passeds);

                push(chk, &stack_of_chunks_in_tangling);
                reverse(&blks);

                Block blk;
                FOREACH(blk, blks) {
                    UNDER(
                        const char *exb = expanded_content_of_block_with_labels(blk, remains);
                        // transform_with_labels(exb, blk->filters, remains);
                        str_extend(cc, exb);
                    );
                }

                pop(&stack_of_chunks_in_tangling);
            );

            if (!remains) {
                break;
            }
            push(car(remains), &passeds);
            remains = cdr(remains);
        } while (1);
        ESCAPE(STRDUP(raw_string(cc), ret));
    );

    return ret;
}

const char *
content_of_chunk_ref(ChunkRef ckr)
{
    return content_of_chunk_with_labels(ckr->chunk, ckr->labels);
}

const char *
expanded_content_of_block_with_labels(Block blk, List lbls)
{
    char *ret;
    UNDER(
        Str ect = new_str();
        List bcs = copy_list(blk->contents);
        reverse(&bcs);

        BlockContent bc;
        FOREACH(bc, bcs) {
            if (bc->type == BLOCK_CONTENT_TEXT) {
                str_extend(ect, bc->value.text);
            }
            if (bc->type == BLOCK_CONTENT_CHUNK_REF) {
                UNDER(
                    List l1, l2, l;

                    l1 = copy_list(lbls);
                    l2 = copy_list(bc->value.chunk.chunk_ref->labels);
                    l = append(&l2, &l1);

                    const char *cc = content_of_chunk_with_labels(
                        bc->value.chunk.chunk_ref->chunk, l
                    );

                    // transform_with_labels(cc, bc->value.chunk.filters, lbls);
                    str_extend(ect, cc);
                );
            }
        }
        ESCAPE(STRDUP(raw_string(ect), ret));
    );

    return ret;
}
@

@<litarfs operations@=
LITARFS_OPER(init),
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
static void *
litarfs_init(struct fuse_conn_info *conn, struct fuse_config *cfg)
{
    (void) conn;
    (void) cfg;
}
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/function declarations@=
static void *litarfs_init(struct fuse_conn_info *conn, struct fuse_config *cfg);
@

# Parsing

The purpose of parsing is to extract relations between objects of
a specific domain from unstructured information.

The grammar of litar is designed deliberately to be as simple as possible.
As a result, the tokens only have four types, control character,
normal character, newline and EOF,
and the whole parsing process can be implemented as a finite state machine.

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/data types@=
typedef struct {
    const char *file;
    FILE *in;
    /* current position, current column, current row */
    int cp, cc, cr;
    /* previous position, previous column, previous row */
    int pp, pc, pr;
} *InputFrame;

typedef struct {
    enum {
        TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER,
        TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER,
        TOKEN_NEWLINE,
        TOKEN_EOF,
    } type;
    char value;
} *Token;
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
static Token next_token(InputFrame ipt)
{
    Token tkn = NULL;
    char c;
    int n = 0;

    if (!ipt->in) {
        if ((ipt->in = fopen(ipt->file, "r")) == NULL) {
            perror(ipt->file);
            longjmp(exit_main, 1);
        }
    }

    NEW0(tkn);

    ipt->pp = ipt->cp;
    ipt->pc = ipt->cc;
    ipt->pr = ipt->cr;

    n += (c = fgetc(ipt->in)) == EOF ? 0 : 1;
    if (c == '\100') {
        n += (c = fgetc(ipt->in)) == EOF ? 0 : 1;
        if (isalpha(c)) {
            n -= ungetc(c, ipt->in) == EOF ? 0 : 1;
            tkn->type = TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER;
            tkn->value = '\100';
        } else {
            tkn->type = TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER;
            if (c == EOF || c == '\n' || c == '\t' || c == ' ') {
                tkn->value = ' ';
            } else {
                tkn->value = c;
            }
            if (c == '\n') {
                n -= ungetc(c, ipt->in) == EOF ? 0 : 1;
            }
        }
    } else if (c == '\n') {
        tkn->type = TOKEN_NEWLINE;
        tkn->value = '\n';
        ipt->cp += n;
        ++ipt->cr;
        ipt->cc = 0;
        n = 0;
    } else if (c == EOF) {
        tkn->type = TOKEN_EOF;
        tkn->value = EOF;
    } else {
        tkn->type = TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER;
        tkn->value = c;
    }
    ipt->cp += n;
    ipt->cc += n;

    return tkn;
}
@

There's a lot of names in a litar archive. To avoid potential miss use of
blanks, the names are normalized according to a simple rule.
The names consists of none-space words, so "name of chunk" does not
differ from "  name  of  chunk".

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
const char *
normalize(const char *name)
{
    int i = 0, j = 0, l = strlen(name);
    char *norm = NULL;

    STRDUP(name, norm);

    l = strlen(name);
    while (i < l) {
        if (isspace(name[i])) {
            if (j != 0 && !isspace(norm[j-1])) {
                norm[j++] = ' ';
            }
        } else {
            norm[j++] = name[i];
        }
        ++i;
    }
    if (isspace(norm[j-1])) {
        norm[j-1] = '\0';
    } else {
        norm[j] = '\0';
    }

    const char *a;
    a = atom_str(norm);

    FREE(norm);

    return a;
}
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/data type declarations@=
@<basic@:light module@:C Structure@/exported data type declarations@>
@<state@:light module@:C Structure@/exported data type declarations@>
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/global variables@=
@<parse@:Light@/declaration of states@>
@

There are some common patterns will be used many times.

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
static State state_parse_text_in(State s, Signal sig)
{
    Str cache = new_str();
    s->local = cache;

    Token tkn;
    RENAME(sig, tkn);
    if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER) {
        str_append(cache, tkn->value);
    }

    return NULL;
}
static State state_parse_text_handler(State s, Signal sig)
{
    Token tkn;
    RENAME(sig, tkn);

    Str cache;
    RENAME(s->local, cache);

    if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '\100') {
        str_append(cache, '\100');
        return s;
    }
    if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER || tkn->type == TOKEN_NEWLINE) {
        str_append(cache, tkn->value);
        return s;
    }
    if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER) {
        return s->parent;
    }
    return NULL;
}
static State state_parse_text_out(State s, Signal sig)
{
    free_str(cache);
    return NULL;
}
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/data types@=
struct state_parse_chunk_ref_local {
    List labels;
    const char *module_name;
    const char *chunk_name;
};
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
static inline bool is_chunk_ref_end(Token tkn)
{
    return tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER
        && (tkn->value == '=' || tkn->value == '>' || tkn->value == ' ' || tkn->value == '|');
}
static State state_parse_chunk_ref_in(State s, Signal sig)
{
    struct state_parse_chunk_ref_local *lc_r;
    NEW0(lc_r);
    s->local = lc_r;
    return NULL;
}
static State state_parse_chunk_ref_handler(State s, Signal sig)
{
    Token tkn;
    RENAME(sig, tkn);
    if (is_chunk_ref_end(tkn)) {
        return s->parent;
    }
    return NULL;
}
static State state_parse_chunk_ref_out(State s, Signal sig)
{
    struct state_parse_chunk_ref_local *lc_r;
    RENAME(s->local, lc_r);
    free_list(lc_r->labels);
    FREE(lc_r);
    return NULL;
}
static State state_parse_chunk_ref_final(State sc, Signal sig)
{
    Str cache;
    struct state_parse_chunk_ref_local *lc_r;
    RENAME(sc->local, cache);
    RENAME(sc->parent->local, lc_r);

    const char *name;
    name = normalize(raw_string(cache));

    Token tkn;
    RENAME(sig, tkn);
    if (
        tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == ':'
        && !tkn->chunk_name && !tkn->module_name
    ) {
        push(name, lc_r->labels);
        return sc;
    }
    if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '/' && !tkn->chunk_name) {
        lc_r->module_name = name;
        return sc;
    }
    if (is_chunk_ref_end(tkn)) {
        lc_r->chunk_name = name;
        return sc;
    }
    return NULL;
}
static void state_parse_chunk_ref_callback(State sc, Signal sig)
{
    Str cache;
    struct state_parse_chunk_ref_local *lc_r;
    RENAME(sc->local, cache);
    RENAME(sc->parent->local, lc_r);

    const char *name;
    name = normalize(raw_string(cache));

    Token tkn;
    RENAME(sig, tkn);
    if (
        tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == ':'
        && !tkn->chunk_name && !tkn->module_name
    ) {
        push(name, lc_r->labels);
    }
    if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '/' && !tkn->chunk_name) {
        lc_r->module_name = name;
    }
    if (is_chunk_ref_end(tkn)) {
        lc_r->chunk_name = name;
    }
}
@

@<parse@:Light@/states@=
{
    name => "compile",
    type => "and",
    has_final => 1,
    has_callback => 1,
},
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/data types@=
struct state_compile_local {
    List input_frames;
    HashTable modules;
    Archive archive;
};
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
static State state_compile_in(State s, Signal sig)
{
    struct state_compile_local *lc_r;
    NEW0(lc_r);
    NEW0(lc_r->archive);
    lc_r->archive->modules = new_string_hash_table(HASH_TABLE_DEFAULT_SIZE);
    s->local = lc_r;
    return NULL;
}

static State state_compile_handler(State s, Signal sig)
{
    Token tkn;
    RENAME(sig, tkn);

    if (tkn->type == TOKEN_EOF) {
        struct state_compile_local *lc_r;
        RENAME(s->local, lc_r);

        InputFrame ipt;
        RENAME(car(lc_r->input_frames), ipt);

        fclose(ipt->in);
        FREE(ipt);
        pop(&lc_r->input_frames);

        return s;
    }
    return NULL;
}

static State state_compile_out(State s, Signal sig)
{
    FREE(s->local);
    return NULL;
}
@

@<litarfs@:light@:C Structure@/functions@=
Archive
parse_archive(const char *archive)
{
    Archive ret;

    @<parse@:Light@/initialization of states@>

    struct state_compile_local *lc_r;
    RENAME(state_compile->local, lc_r);

    do {
        char path[PATH_MAX+1];
        char *ptr = realpath(archive, path);
        if (path == NULL) {
            perror("realpath");
            longjmp(exit_main, 1);
        }
        InputFrame ipt = NULL;
        NEW0(ipt);
        ipt->file = atom_str(path);
        push(ipt, &lc_r->input_frames);
    } while (0);

    while (lc_r->input_frames) {
        Token tkn;
        InputFrame ipt;

        RENAME(car(lc_r->input_frames), ipt);

        UNDER(
            tkn = next_token(ipt);
#ifdef SHOW_COMPILE_INFO
            if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER) {
                fprintf(stderr, "Handling control character %c\n", tkn->value);
            } else if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER) {
                fprintf(stderr, "Handling normal character %c\n", tkn->value);
            } else if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NEWLINE) {
                fprintf(stderr, "Handling newline\n");
            } else if (tkn->type == TOKEN_EOF) {
                fprintf(stderr, "Handling EOF\n");
            }
#endif
            ESCAPE(
                if (!state_handle_signal(state_compile, tkn)) {
                    fprintf(
                        stderr,
                        "Unexpected token at row %d column %d of %s\n",
                        ipt->pr+1, ipt->pc+1, ipt->file
                    );
                    longjmp(exit_main, 1);
                }
            );
        );
    }
    ret = lc_r->archive;

    @<parse@:Light@/cleaning of states@>
    return ret;
}
@

@<build context for "fuse_main"@=
HashTable modules;
modules = parse_archive(user_options.archive);
longjmp(exit_main, 2);
@

